course: everyday
lesson: 354
title: Rude words at the table
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Charlie heard a rude word on the school bus in Manchester and said it at Sunday dinner to see what would happen. Grandma put her fork down and went quiet; Mum looked shocked and sent him from the table. He sat on the stairs feeling silly — he did not even know exactly what it meant. He came back, said sorry to Grandma, and she told him «Thank you, love. Some words hurt people even if you don't know why».
explain: When Mum said no to a sleepover, Zoe shouted «I hate you!» and slammed her door. Mum did not shout back; she just went quiet. Zoe could not sleep for thinking about Mum's face. She crept downstairs, said sorry and that she loved her, and Mum said «I know. I love you too. The answer is still no.»
explain: Words at home, at school and on the bus land on real people. Rudeness to grown-ups who look after you — teachers, dinner staff, drivers, a stepmum, Grandad — hurts them even when they do not show it.
explain: At school Jack rolled his eyes and said «whatever» to his teacher to make the class laugh; the class went quiet and he had to stay behind. Dan answered back to the dinner lady; she told his teacher and he lost his playtime. At home Ella told her stepmum «you're not my mum» and saw how hurt she was; later she said sorry and drew her a picture. Sophie called Grandad's stories boring and he stopped telling them — until she said sorry and asked him for one.
explain: The consequence varies: sometimes a look, sometimes leaving the table, staying behind or a visit to the head teacher. But the putting right is always yours: a real sorry, and choosing other words next time.
fact: Some words are hurtful or offensive even if the child saying them does not know their meaning.
fact: Rolling your eyes and saying «whatever» is a way of being rude without swear words.
fact: Words said in anger, like «I hate you», can hurt more than you mean them to.
fact: Little children copy the words they hear older children use.
fact: Dinner staff, bus drivers and lollipop people are doing a job that helps you, and deserve respect.
fact: A stepparent who looks after you can be deeply hurt by «you're not my mum».
fact: A rude sign behind someone's back is still rude, even if they do not see it.
fact: Older people's stories often hold family history that you can only learn from them.
fact: Saying sorry soon after a rude word helps more than waiting days.
fact: When you are cross, you can choose a harmless word or walk away for a moment instead of swearing.
exercise: Why did Charlie say the rude word at dinner? | To show off and see what would happen | Because Grandma asked him to | Because it was a spelling word | To upset Mum on purpose
exercise: Charlie did not know what the word meant. Could it still hurt Grandma? | Yes, some words hurt whatever the speaker knows | No, words only hurt if the person saying them means them | No, grandmas don't mind words | Only if he said it twice
exercise: What put Charlie's rude word right? | Saying sorry to Grandma and not using it again | Explaining that a big boy at school said it to him first | Saying it more quietly next time | Pretending he said a different word
exercise: Zoe shouted «I hate you» when Mum said no. Which feeling made her say it? | Anger | Hunger | Happiness | Boredom
exercise: Why did Zoe lie awake afterwards? | She knew her angry words had hurt Mum | Mum had taken her pillow | She was planning what to shout next | She had drunk too much juice
exercise: What did Zoe do to put it right? | Said sorry and told Mum she loved her | Waited for Mum to say sorry first | Asked again for the sleepover | Slammed the door again to finish it
exercise: Jack said «whatever» to his teacher and rolled his eyes to make the class laugh. What happened? | The class went quiet and he had to stay behind | The whole class cheered him | The teacher laughed too | Nothing at all, because eye-rolling is a polite way to answer
exercise: Mia swore when she dropped her bag, and a little girl nearby copied her. Why does that matter? | Little children copy words they hear | Little girls never copy anyone | Swearing makes it hurt less | Swearing helps bags not break
exercise: What could Mia say next time she is cross? | A harmless word, or nothing at all | A louder swear word | The same word, but whispered | A word in another language
exercise: Dan answered back to the dinner lady who said he could not go out yet. What was the consequence? | She told his teacher and he lost playtime | He was allowed out first | The dinner lady said sorry to him | He was given extra pudding for standing up for himself
exercise: Why should Dan respect the dinner lady? | She is doing a job that looks after the children | She is older than his mum | She chooses the school menu | She can give out prizes
exercise: Ella told her stepmum «you're not my mum». What did her stepmum feel? | Hurt, because she looks after Ella | Pleased, because it was good to finally hear the truth | Nothing, stepmums never mind | Happy to have a break
exercise: What put things right between Ella and her stepmum? | Saying sorry and drawing her a picture | Telling Dad that what she had said was true anyway | Going to her room for a week | Asking for a new stepmum
exercise: Ryan made a rude sign behind his teacher's back. She did not see it. Was it still wrong? | Yes, rude is rude even if unseen | No, it only counts if she sees | No, signs are not words | Only if the head teacher sees it, because then it counts
exercise: Sophie told Grandad his stories were boring, and he stopped telling them. What would put it right? | Saying sorry and asking him to tell one | Telling him to make them shorter | Asking him for a go on his tablet instead of a story | Saying Grandma's stories are better
exercise: Charlie's friend says «I said it at my house last week and got away with it». What is true? | Getting away with it doesn't make it kind | It proves the word is fine to say at anybody's house | It means grandparents don't care | It means only Charlie's family minds
test: heard a new word on the bus, gonna say it at dinner and see what nan does lol. Good idea? | No, it may hurt her and spoil dinner | Yes, nans love new words | Yes, nan might laugh | Only if Mum is out
test: Mum says no to a sleepover. Kid screams «I HATE YOU». Mum goes quiet. What's she most likely feeling? | Hurt, even though she knows it's anger | Glad, because the kid finally said what they really feel | Bored of the talk | Angry enough to say yes
test: teacher: «Did I just see you roll your eyes at me?» Best thing to say? | «Yes, sorry, that was rude» | «No, my eyes just do that when I'm tired, Miss» | «Whatever» | «Everyone does it»
test: kid in the playground: «oh sh— !» and the reception kid next to him goes «sh—!» too. What's the lesson? | Little ones copy what they hear | Reception kids already know every word | It doesn't matter in a playground | Only their parents' words get copied
test: bus driver takes forever, ur mate shouts «get a move on, grandad!». You laugh. Best move next day? | Say sorry to the driver | Shout it again, even louder | Pretend it wasn't you at all | Tell the driver he is slow
test: «she's not my real mum so i don't have to listen». What's closer to the truth? | She cares for you, and the words hurt her | Stepmums have no say at all about what happens at home | She'll never mind what you say | Only real mums get hurt
test: said «whatever» to a teacher last year and nothing happened, «it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, it's still rude, and next time may not go so well | Yes, if it worked once it's fine | Yes, teachers don't really hear it | Only to supply teachers
test: «Grandad, that story AGAIN? Booooring.» Grandad goes quiet. What's the kind fix? | Say sorry and ask him to tell it again | Leave the room | Tell him to tell a new one, because that one is boring | Turn the TV up
test: Dad, angry: «Go to your room and think about what you said». After an hour, what's the wise step? | Come down and say sorry | Stay there until he apologises | Climb out of the bedroom window | Shout at him through the door
test: dropped my bag in the rain and swore and a mum glared. What could I say next time? | «Oh no!» or nothing at all | A worse word | The same word again, but said a bit more quietly | A word she won't know
test: friend: «I made a rude sign at Mr Price, he didn't even see». What's true? | It's still rude, seen or not | It doesn't count because he never saw it | It's only rude if he's a nice teacher | It's fine if it's behind his back
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a word of unknown meaning may nonetheless cause offence when uttered. | Its offensiveness depends on the listener and not on the speaker's knowledge | Words can cause offence only when the speaker knows precisely what they mean | Words uttered at mealtimes cannot offend | Offence is caused solely by volume
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the utterance of «I hate you» to a parent in a moment of anger? | It may cause hurt greater than the speaker intends | It is harmless because it is not meant | It is an acceptable means of persuasion | It obliges the parent to reverse the refusal in order to restore calm at home
exam: It is given that young children commonly imitate the language of older children. Select the response consistent with this fact. | Older children ought to choose their words with care in their presence | Young children are unable to learn any words merely by hearing them spoken | Imitation applies only to written language | The use of rude words is therefore harmless
exam: State the measure most consistent with making amends for discourtesy to a member of school staff. | A prompt apology in person and courteous conduct thereafter | A written complaint about the member of staff | Avoidance of the staff member indefinitely | An explanation to the staff member that others had been equally discourteous
